Introduction
Glioblastoma (GBM) is the most common primary malignant neoplasm of the
central nervous system, continues to carry a poor prognosis, with a 5-year survival rate
below 10% despite recent therapeutic advances. A significant proportion of patients
experience tumor recurrence during the early phases of radiotherapy [1,2], and over 80%
of recurrence occurs at the surgical residual margin [3], where infiltrative tumor cells
invade non-enhancing regions typically excluded from clinical target volumes (CTV)
in conventional radiotherapy planning[4,5].
Current diagnostic and therapeutic monitoring strategies rely heavily on
conventional magnetic resonance imaging (MRI), particularly contrast-enhanced T1
weighted imaging (CE-T1WI) and T2-fluid-attenuated inversion recovery (FLAIR)
sequences. Although CE-T1WI delineates enhancing tumor bulk effectively, it fails to
discriminate non-enhancing tumor infiltration from vasogenic edema in perilesional
T2/FLAIR hyperintense regions. Moreover, microscopic tumor invasion often extends
beyond radiologically visible abnormalities [6], limiting the ability of conventional
MRI to detect early recurrence. Precise identification of high-risk patients could
significantly improve personalized postoperative management.
Artificial intelligence (AI)-driven methods integrating multiparametric MRI with
machine learning have shown promise in predicting early recurrence and delineating
tumor invasion range [7-15]. However, critical limitations remain: (1) small sample
sizes in existing studies increase overfitting risks [16,17], and heavy reliance on
advanced imaging modalities impairs clinical applicability; (2) most research focuses
on preoperative imaging, neglecting the complex postoperative microenvironment,
which includes a mixture of edema, gliosis, and residual non-enhancing tumor tissue
[18]. Additionally, anatomical shifts between pre- and postoperative imaging
complicate spatial registration and diminish model reliability [10,11].
Recent advances in multi-task deep learning have demonstrated superior
performance in preoperative tumor segmentation, molecular profiling, and grading of
gliomas and other tumors [19,20]. These automated pipelines allow for rapid, non
invasive acquisition of imaging biomarkers and assist clinical decision-making.
Extending this paradigm to the postoperative setting is critical for optimizing
radiotherapy protocols, treatment planning, and follow-up strategies. An integrated
system performing automated segmentation, risk stratification, and spatial mapping of
recurrence-prone regions could redefine CTV delineation and enable personalized
precision therapy [21].
Building upon these advances, we developed DeepGBM-Recure, an intelligent
deep learning system translating routine postoperative MRI into actionable
radiotherapy guidance. It uniquely integrates: (1) automated segmentation of peri
cavitary hyperintensities; (2) early recurrence prediction for risk-adapted surveillance;
and (3) voxel-level spatial mapping to guide precision dose painting. This integrated
system can enable a seamless transition from routine MRI data to clinically actionable
guidance without the need for manual intervention.
Materials and Methods


4
Study Population
This retrospective multicenter study was approved by the Ethics Committee of
West China Hospital, Sichuan University (NO.20251449), with a waiver for written
informed consent. GBM data were collected from four centers: one internal cohort
(West China Hospital, WCH) and three public datasets from The Cancer Imaging
Archive (TCIA), including the Ivy Glioblastoma Atlas Project (Ivy GAP) [22,23], the
Burdenko Glioblastoma Progression Dataset (BGPD) [24], and the Río Hortega
University Hospital Glioblastoma Dataset (RHUH-GBM) [25]. The inclusion criteria
were as follows: (1) histopathologically confirmed newly diagnosed GBM according
to contemporary WHO classification standards at the time of resection; (2) gross total,
near total or subtotal resection of contrast-enhancing tumor components; (3) availability
of postoperative MRI sequences, including pre-recurrence FLAIR images and CE
T1WI images at the time of recurrence. Patients were excluded if they exhibited distant
recurrence, cerebrospinal fluid dissemination, or significant image artifacts. A detailed
overview of datasets distribution and inclusion/exclusion criteria is presented in
Supplementary Fig. S1. Early recurrence was defined as tumor progression within six
months postoperatively, confirmed either by histopathology or based on mRANO
criteria [26]. The full dataset was divided into three subsets: a train and validation
cohort (derived from WCH and RHUH-GBM) for model development and internal
validation, and an independent test cohort (from Ivy GAP and BGPD) for evaluating
the model’s performance in automated segmentation, early recurrence prediction and
spatially mapping high-risk recurrence areas in GBM.
MRI Acquisitions
MRI examinations were performed using 1.5T and 3.0T scanners from multiple
vendors, including GE, Siemens, Toshiba, Philips, and UIH, across participating
centers. Primary imaging parameters varied across scanner platforms. A detailed
summary of MRI acquisition parameters for the train, validation, and test sets is
provided in Supplementary Table S1.
Data Preprocessing and Delineation
All Digital Imaging and Communications in Medicine (DICOM) files were
converted to Neuroimaging Informatics Technology Initiative (NIfTI) format. CE
T1WI images at recurrence were rigidly registered to the corresponding pre-recurrence
FLAIR images. Intensity normalization was applied to harmonize grayscale
distributions across scanners, followed by isotropic resampling to a voxel resolution of
1×1×1 mm3 to ensure spatial consistency.
A neuroradiologist (Y. Li, 5 years of experience) manually segmented two regions
of interest using ITK-SNAP software (version 3.8.0): (1) enhancing tumor regions on
recurrence CE-T1WI and (2) peri-cavitary hyperintense signal zones on postoperative
FLAIR images. All segmentations were subsequently reviewed and refined by a senior
neuroradiologist (S. Lui, >10 years of experience) to ensure consistency and accuracy.
Development of DeepGBM-Recure
We developed and validated DeepGBM-Recure, an integrated deep learning


5
system composed of three synergistic modules: a peri-cavity hyperintensities
segmentation model (referred to as the auto-seg model), a patient-level recurrence
prediction model, and a voxel-level recurrence prediction and spatial mapping model.
The overall study workflow is shown in Fig. 1A.
Hyperintensities Segmentation Model
We first trained a 3D MRI segmentation model, named auto-seg model, based on
the nnU-Net framework implemented in PyTorch (version 1.12) [27]. The model was
trained using postoperative FLAIR images from the train and validation sets to
automatically delineates peri-cavity hyperintense regions. It addressed the complexity
of postoperative brains through adaptive kernel size selection and cascade architectures.
Segmentation performance was validated on the test set using Dice similarity
coefficient.
Patient-Level Prediction Model
Radiomic features were extracted from automatically segmented peri-cavity
regions on postoperative FLAIR images using Pyradiomics library (version 3.0.1)
[28,29]. Following a three-step feature selection pipeline (Mann-Whitney U test,
Pearson correlation analysis, and SHAP-based importance ranking), the top-10
prognostic features were used to train and compare eight machine learning classifiers
for predicting early recurrence. The optimal classifier, selected based on validation set
performance, was designated as the final patient-level model and evaluated on an
independent test set. Detailed radiomics analysis information is provided in
Supplementary Material A.1.
Voxel-Level Prediction Model
Based on the 3D volume of interest (VOI) obtained from the auto-seg model, we
further applied the Simple Linear Iterative Clustering (SLIC) algorithm to partition the
VOI into 9×9×9 supervoxels [30]. Each VOI could be divided into thousands of
supervoxel objects. The recurrence regions from follow-up CE-T1WI were registered
to baseline FLAIR, and each supervoxel was labeled as recurrent or non-recurrent
accordingly. Radiomic features were then extracted from each supervoxel object,
followed by a three-stage feature selection process and modeling using eight classifiers
on the train set. The best-performing voxel-level model was selected based on
validation set results. The algorithmic pipeline was consistent with that of the patient
level model. Based on the voxel-level model, we assigned a recurrence probability to
each supervoxel, and high-risk recurrence regions were visualized as probability
heatmaps overlaid on the FLAIR images.
Machine Learning Classifier Implementation
Eight machine learning classifiers were implemented using the Python Scikit-learn
library (version 0.24.2), including logistic regression (LR), support vector machine
(SVM), decision tree (DT), random forest (RF), gradient boosting (GB), extreme
gradient boosting (XGBoost), light gradient boosting machine (LightGBM), and Neural
Network multilayer perceptron (MLP). The optimal classifier was compared and
selected based on five-fold cross-validation on the train set and performance on the


6
validation set. The final selected model was then evaluated on the independent test set.
Clinical Deployment and Inference
The DeepGBM-Recure system integrates clinical inference by processing
postoperative FLAIR images through its three core components: 1) automated
segmentation, 2) patient-level recurrence prediction, and 3) voxel-level spatial
localization. This workflow generates two key outputs including a personalized early
recurrence probability score (0-1 scale) and a high-risk recurrence heatmap. These
outputs are integrated into a Clinical Decision Support (CDS). These reports
incorporate risk-adapted follow-up recommendations, evidence-based treatment
strategies, and optimized radiotherapy targets. Finalized outputs are seamlessly
delivered to clinician workstations (e.g., PACS/RIS terminals) to support real-time
clinical implementation (see Fig. 1B).
Statistical Analysis
Statistical analyses were performed using SPSS (version 22.0; IBM Corp.,
Armonk, NY, USA) and Python (version 3.8). Comparisons of clinical characteristics
were performed using Pearson's chi-squared test, the Kruskal-Wallis rank sum test, or
Fisher's exact test, depending on variable type. The Mann-Whitney U test was used to
evaluate the association between radiomic features and the predictive variable, while
the Pearson correlation test was used to assess the correlations among features. The
performance of hyperintensity segmentation model was evaluated using the Dice
similarity coefficient. For recurrence prediction tasks, receiver operating characteristic
(ROC) curves were generated to evaluate overall model performance. Quantitative
metrics including the area under the ROC curve (AUC), accuracy, precision, recall, and
F1-score were calculated. The DeLong test was used to evaluate statistical differences
in AUC between different classifiers. Model interpretability was evaluated using
SHapley Additive exPlanations (SHAP) feature importance rankings for both patient
level and voxel-level prediction models. Calibration curves and decision curve analyses
were employed to assess the accuracy of predicted recurrence probabilities and the
clinical utility. Finally, voxel-level model's performance was visualized via parametric
heatmaps, which illustrated predicted high-risk recurrence zones surrounding the
surgical cavity. Statistical significance was defined as a P-value < 0.05.
Results
A total of 184 patients were enrolled in this study (118 males, 64%; mean age 55
± 12 years), including 110 from the WCH cohort, 35 from the RHUH-GBM dataset, 22
from the Ivy GAP, and 17 from the BGPD. Patients from the WCH and RHUH-GBM
cohorts were randomly assigned to train (n=116; 84 males, 72%; mean age 56 ± 11
years) and validation (n=29; 15 males, 52%; mean age 52 ± 14 years) sets via an 8:2
stratified sampling ratio. The independent test set consisted of 39 patients from Ivy
GAP and BGPD (19 males, 49%; mean age 55 ± 14 years). The demographic and
clinical characteristics of the train, validation, and test sets are summarized in Table 1.
A comparison of early-recurrence versus non-early-recurrence subgroups is provided
in Supplementary Table S2.


7
From these 184 patients, a total of 5,006,961 supervoxels were extracted from
hyperintense signal VOIs surrounding the surgical cavities. These supervoxels were
also stratified into train (n=3,031,275), validation (n=1,299,119), and test sets
(n=665,567) based on patient-level groupings (see Supplementary Fig. S2).
Across the train, validation, and test sets, the auto-seg model yielded mean Dice
coefficients of 0.93 ± 0.03, 0.89 ± 0.07, and 0.85 ± 0.09, respectively (Supplementary
Fig. S3).
Prediction metrics and ROC curves for all machine learning classifiers in the train
and validation sets at patient-level and voxel-level models are summarized in
Supplementary Table S3 and Fig. S4. Among the classifiers, the RF modal achieved
the highest predictive performance (P < 0.05). SHAP summary plots (Supplementary
Fig. S5) revealed the contribution of the top-10 radiomic features to the RF model’s
output, enhancing model interpretability. Detailed definitions and biological
interpretations of these features F1-F10 are provided in Supplementary Table S4 and
S5.
On the independent test set, the patient-level and voxel-level models achieved the
following performance, an AUC of 0.76 and 0.80, accuracy of 0.69 and 0.79, precision
of 0.63 and 0.78, recall of 0.95 and 0.80, and F1-score of 0.76 and 0.79 respectively
(Table 2). ROC curves for both models across the three study sets are shown in Fig. 2A
and 2B. The calibration curve for the patient-level model demonstrated strong
agreement between predictive and observed outcomes, with a mean absolute error of
0.034 (Fig. 2C). Decision curve analysis further confirmed the model’s clinical utility,
showing net benefit across a broad range of thresholds probabilities (Fig. 2D).
Representative voxel-level predictions from the test set are shown in Fig. 3.
Heatmaps depicting high-risk recurrence regions showed strong spatial concordance
with actual recurrence locations. And the normalized histograms of voxel-based
prediction probabilities are provided in Supplementary Fig. S6. However, a subset of
cases exhibited spatial discrepancies between predicted and observed recurrence areas
due to postoperative morphological alterations of the surgical cavity (Supplementary
Fig. S7).
Given that 45% of patients in our study had unknown MGMT methylation status,
we conducted additional subgroup analyses of MGMT-known versus MGMT-unknown
patients in the test cohort to verify the model's robust predictive capability regardless
of MGMT status. The results demonstrate that the model’s performance is not
influenced by MGMT status, supporting its role as an imaging-based AI tool capable
of achieving reliable prediction (Supplementary Fig. S8).
We further conducted a hypothetical clinical deployment and case-based analysis
to illustrate the potential utility of DeepGBM-Recure. Fig. 4 demonstrates
representative cases of early and non-early recurrence predicted by the system during
clinical application. Case W45 involved a 63-year-old female with unknown IDH status
and MGMT promoter methylation, while case Burdenko-GBM-032 involved a 55-year
old female with IDH-wildtype glioblastoma and undetermined MGMT promoter status.
The patient-level model predicted recurrence probabilities of 0.74 and 0.31 for both
cases, respectively. Voxel-level probability heatmaps revealed high-risk subregions


8
that corresponded closely with the actual recurrence sites, which were confirmed at 2
and 44 months postoperatively, demonstrating strong spatial concordance between
model predictions and clinical outcomes. As a demonstration of how the model's
outputs could inform clinical decision-making: for case W45 (high predicted risk),
more intensive follow-up intervals could be considered, along with potential enrollment
in trials exploring combined targeted/immunotherapeutic regimens. For case
Burdenko-GBM-032 (low predicted risk), maintaining the standard Stupp protocol with
standard or extended surveillance intervals might be justified. Furthermore, a
hypothetical radiotherapy plans was generated to illustrate how voxel-level heatmaps
could guide precision dose painting, suggesting dose escalation to high-risk subregions
within acceptable safety limits while maintaining standard dosing in low-risk areas. It
is critical to emphasize that these management strategies were not implemented in this
retrospective cohort but are presented here solely as examples of potential clinical
applications.
Discussion
In this study, we developed and evaluated DeepGBM-Recure, the first robust
integrated AI system that unifies automated segmentation, patient-level recurrence
prediction, and voxel-wise spatial localization for comprehensive postoperative
management of GBM. This synergistic triad establishes an efficient clinical workflow,
where nnU-Net based segmentation provides standardized anatomical inputs, patient
level risk stratification supports personalized surveillance planning, and voxel-level
spatial mapping informs precision radiotherapy, all while minimizing manual
intervention and enhancing therapeutic precision.
DeepGBM-Recure demonstrated strong performance across all modules. The
segmentation model achieved a Dice coefficient of 0.85 ± 0.09 in the test set; the
patient-level model yielded an AUC of 0.76 for early recurrence risk stratification; and
the voxel-level model achieved an AUC of 0.80, with notable spatial concordance
between predicted high-risk zones and actual recurrence regions. Through rigorous
validation and representative clinical application, the system has proven to be an
accurate, robust, and interpretable AI solution for postoperative GBM management.
Prior radiomic studies have primarily focused on single-scale recurrence
prediction. For example, Cepeda et al. [31] developed a voxel-level model using
postoperative multiparametric MRI (AUC=0.81 in n=15 test cases), though the small
sample size (n=55) raised concerns about overfitting. Similarly, Akbari et al. [15]
constructed a model using preoperative multiparametric MRI to delineate GBM
infiltration zones, achieving AUCs of 0.80 and 0.84 in the training (n=31) and
validation (n=34) cohorts, respectively. However, as previously noted, beyond risks of
overfitting due to limited sample size, preoperative MRI-based predictions face
additional challenges: postoperative anatomical shifts in peri-cavitary regions increase
co-registration complexity between preoperative scans and recurrence imaging,
frequently resulting in spatial mismatches between predicted and actual recurrence sites.
This discordance complicates model validation and clinical interpretation. In addition,
other similar studies have also reported significant findings. However, except for the


9
research by Yan, Hu, and Cepeda et al. [31-33], the data in the remaining studies were
derived from single-center cohorts [8-13,15,34,35]. The lack of external validation
undermines the generalizability and reproducibility of these models, posing a barrier to
clinical translation.
MRI-derived radiomic phenotypes have recently gained attention as promising
surrogate biomarkers for GBM prognosis [36-38], although their biological
interpretability remains insufficiently explored. In our study, ten radiomic features were
separately selected for constructing both patient-level and voxel-level models. These
features predominantly capture the complex biological landscape surrounding the
postoperative residual cavity, which often includes necrosis, edema, viable tumor
remnants, and treatment response. Among the top five most important features in the
patient-level model, F1 represents low-intensity gray-level regions in original images,
possibly indicating necrosis or cystic changes. A lower F1 may reflect more extensive
necrotic, potentially signaling a therapeutic response but also harboring residual tumor
cells that contribute early recurrence. F2 quantifies complex gray-level dependence
structures near the cavity, higher entropy values may reflect microstructural
heterogeneity consistent with infiltrative tumor behavior [39]. F3 captures high
intensity signals, possibly linked to hypermetabolic tumor activity or blood-brain
barrier disruption. F4 may correlate with irregular enhancing margins or microvascular
proliferation, while F5 characterized by high Imc1 values, indicates a combination of
textural regularity and heterogeneity, potentially representing disorganized tissue
architecture such as irregular tumor cell distribution [40]. Collectively, these features
encapsulate peri-cavitary abnormal signal regions from multiple perspectives,
including intensity, texture, and spatial heterogeneity. They offer a quantitative
objective basis for identifying early recurrence, optimizing radiotherapy targets, and
stratifying patient prognosis, potentially reducing reliance on subjective imaging
interpretations. Future research should focus on integrating multimodal data and
validating the predictive value of feature combinations through large-scale, prospective
multicenter trials.
In addition, we observed that the important features differ between the patient
level and voxel-level models, which can be attributed to the differences in spatial scales
of the analysis targets. This observation underscores the inherent spatial heterogeneity
within peri-tumor regions. By employing our proposed radiomics-based dual-model
strategy, recurrence-related information can be effectively extracted and characterized
from both global (patient-level) and local (supervoxel-level) perspectives, enabling
multi-scale prediction of recurrence risk in GBM patients.
Among the various machine learning classifiers tested, the RF algorithm achieved
the best predictive performance at both the patient and voxel levels. This result may be
attributed to the RF algorithm's capacity to capture complex, non-linear patterns in
high-dimensional biomedical data which is consistent with previous studies [29]. In
addition, the incorporation of SHAP analysis enhanced model interpretability, and the
integration of recurrence probability heatmap visualization further improved the
transparency and trustworthiness of the system for clinicians [20,41]. These features
collectively support the deployment of the system in real-world clinical settings and


10
may facilitate broader adoption. For patients stratified as high-risk for early recurrence
by the system, follow-up intervals are recommended to be shortened, incorporating
advanced MRI sequences for focused surveillance of predicted high-risk zones. During
adjuvant chemotherapy, alternative regimens such as dose-dense temozolomide or
inclusion in clinical trials involving targeted/immunotherapy may be considered. At
recurrence monitoring phases, early intervention is recommended over awaiting clinical
deterioration. Dose painting may be implemented for high-risk subregions within safety
constraints guided by probability heatmaps [42,43]. However, treatment options for
early recurrence of glioblastoma are limited. DeepGBM-Recure is only a CDS tool
designed to provide evidence-based information to assist clinicians, not to mandate any
specific treatment plan. The final treatment decisions should be made by the clinical
team according to the individual circumstances, physical condition, and available
resources of patients.
This study has several limitations that should be acknowledged. Firstly, although
we utilized a multicenter dataset with one of the largest sample sizes reported in
comparable studies, the retrospective design and relatively modest cohort size may
restrict the generalizability of findings. Furthermore, heterogeneity in MRI scanners
and imaging protocols across institutions may affect the model’s external validity.
Secondly, in certain cases, ill-defined boundaries between surgical cavities and peri
cavity hyperintense signals on early postoperative FLAIR images led to uncertainties
in ROI delineation. Postoperative anatomical shifts (e.g., cavity contraction and
displacement of surrounding brain tissue) may also cause spatial mismatches between
predicted and observed recurrence locations, thereby compromising voxel-level
prediction accuracy. Future studies may consider employing deformable image
registration algorithms to better account for such brain shifts. In addition, our analysis
was restricted to single-timepoint postoperative FLAIR imaging. The absence of
longitudinal, multi-timepoint data precludes evaluation of dynamic signal evolution in
peri-cavitary regions—a potentially valuable biomarker for recurrence risk
stratification. Further investigation should explore integrating temporal imaging
features to enhance prognostic accuracy and provide deeper insights into recurrence
kinetics. Meanwhile, future research could develop fused models by incorporating
preoperative radiomic features with longitudinal postoperative radiomic features,
thereby further enhancing predictive performance. Additionally, incorporating broader
patient data will comprehensively assess system performance across patient subgroups.
Finally, while our model currently focuses exclusively on postoperative MRI radiomics
to ensure broad applicability, future work should integrate multimodal data streams
(e.g., molecular markers, pathomics) where feasible as data completeness improves.
Conclusion
This study establishes DeepGBM-Recure, the first fully integrated deep learning
system that unifies automated segmentation of postoperative anatomy, patient-level
recurrence prediction, and voxel-wise spatial mapping for glioblastoma management.
By eliminating manual delineation and synergistically integrating recurrence risk
stratification with spatial localization of recurrence hotspots, our approach delivers a


11
comprehensive solution for precision neuro-oncology. Future directions will include
validate in prospective multi-center trials and the development of real-time clinical
interfaces to enable automated radiotherapy targeting and adaptive surveillance,
translating computational innovations into actionable strategies at the bedside.
